<section class="section">
  <div class="section-header reveal">
    <h1>Reading Order</h1>
    <p>The Architecture of Survival spans 3,200 years, from Bronze Age Egypt to the present day. Every book stands alone &mdash; so the real question isn't <em>what order</em>, it's <em>which door</em>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="concept-section reveal">
    <h2>The short answer: pick a thread</h2>
    <p>The books group into three threads. Each one keeps you in a single world, and each one is a complete reading experience on its own. Start wherever your taste points.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="concept-section reveal">
    <h3>The Modern Thread &mdash; 20th century to now</h3>
    <p><em>If you read Kate Quinn, Kristin Hannah, or Michael Crichton.</em> Real archival history and the science that grew out of it.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong><a href="/nobody-lied-to-her/">Nobody Lied to Her</a></strong> &mdash; Berlin &amp; America, 1927&ndash;1955. The American law the Nazis copied, and the family that got sorted by it.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0GPM973N1">The Genesis Protocol</a></strong> &mdash; 2019&ndash;2025. A geneticist, an engineered bioweapon, and 72 hours.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="form-note">These two are the closest thing the series has to a direct sequel pair &mdash; the second picks up a thread the first leaves deliberately unfinished.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="concept-section reveal">
    <h3>The Medieval Thread &mdash; the age of coded manuscripts</h3>
    <p><em>If you read Umberto Eco, Ken Follett, or Dan Brown.</em> Conspiracy, cipher, and the church.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong><a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0HDK6SGM3">The Split Pomegranate</a></strong> &mdash; Damascus &amp; Jerusalem, 1140&ndash;1187. The Crusades, and a love written in coded seals.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0GJTGCHS2">The Aethelred Cipher</a></strong> &mdash; Medieval Germany, 1347. The Black Death, an iron key, a hunted scribe.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div class="concept-section reveal">
    <h3>The Ancient World Thread &mdash; where it starts</h3>
    <p><em>If you read Sue Monk Kidd, Anita Diamant, or Wilbur Smith.</em> The origin of the whole conflict, and the centuries that carried it.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong><a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0GSWLM1WV">The First Key</a></strong> &mdash; Bronze Age Egypt, 1177 BCE. The origin of everything.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0GX2TG21Y">Love Remembers in Silence</a></strong> &mdash; Roman Judea, 26&ndash;42 CE. Mary of Magdala's buried gospel.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0H34DZVXS">What Augustine Hid</a></strong> &mdash; North Africa, 387&ndash;430 CE. A bishop, a courier, and a prophecy of collapse.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="form-note">Finished this thread and want to see where the argument lands? <em>What Augustine Hid</em> and <a href="/nobody-lied-to-her/"><em>Nobody Lied to Her</em></a> ask the same question fifteen centuries apart: what does an institution choose to keep, and what does it let burn?</p>
  </div>

  <div class="concept-section reveal">
    <h2>Publication order</h2>
    <p>This is how the series was designed to unfold &mdash; the era jumps are the point, and each book recontextualizes the last:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>The Aethelred Cipher</strong> &mdash; 1347 CE</li>
      <li><strong>The Genesis Protocol</strong> &mdash; 2019&ndash;2025</li>
      <li><strong>The First Key</strong> &mdash; 1177 BCE</li>
      <li><strong>Love Remembers in Silence</strong> &mdash; 26&ndash;42 CE</li>
      <li><strong>What Augustine Hid</strong> &mdash; 387&ndash;430 CE</li>
      <li><strong>The Split Pomegranate</strong> &mdash; 1140&ndash;1187 CE</li>
      <li><strong>Nobody Lied to Her</strong> &mdash; 1927&ndash;1955</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div class="concept-section reveal">
    <h2>Chronological order</h2>
    <p>For readers who want to travel time in a straight line:</p>
    <p><em>The First Key</em> (1177 BCE) &rarr; <em>Love Remembers in Silence</em> (26 CE) &rarr; <em>What Augustine Hid</em> (410 CE) &rarr; <em>The Split Pomegranate</em> (1140 CE) &rarr; <em>The Aethelred Cipher</em> (1347 CE) &rarr; <em>Nobody Lied to Her</em> (1927) &rarr; <em>The Genesis Protocol</em> (today).</p>
    <p>It works &mdash; but you'll meet a few revelations before the books that set them up.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="concept-section reveal">
    <h2>Do I have to read them in order?</h2>
    <p>No. <strong>Every book stands alone</strong> &mdash; a complete story, in its own era, with its own protagonist, and no cliffhanger that forces the next purchase. The connections reward series readers; they're never required.</p>
  </div>

